  data:  # 104c
    kind: invariant  # 104c
    title: "Entrypoint detection double-counts scripts that have both 'if __name__ == \"__main__\"' AND a main() function (17 main_function entries, 11 distinct scripts on self-analysis)"  # 104c
    status: violated  # 104c
    priority: 2  # 104c
    parent:  # 104c
    tags:  # 104c
    - entrypoint  # 104c
    - deduplication  # 104c
    - self_analysis  # 104c
    - dogfooding  # 104c
    isbefore: []  # 104c
    duplicate_of: []  # 104c
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 104c
    pr_ref:  # 104c
    description: "**Found via self-analysis dogfooding on 2026-05-16 (round 9).**\n\n**Symptom.** Hypergumbo's self-analysis reports 17 `main_function` entrypoints, but only 11 distinct executable scripts exist. 6 scripts are double-counted because the entrypoint detector emits BOTH:\n- The module-level `if __name__ == \"__main__\"` block (`kind=main_function, label=\"Python script (if __name__ == '__main__')\"`, on the module Symbol)\n- The `main()` function the block calls (`kind=main_function, label=\"Python main()\"`, on the function Symbol)\n\nConcrete duplicates on self-analysis (each appears twice):\n\n| Script | Module entrypoint | main() entrypoint |\n|---|---|---|\n| `scripts/compute_probe_embeddings.py` | `<module:compute_probe_embeddings.py>` | `main:function` |\n| `scripts/hypergumbo_diag.py` | `<module:hypergumbo_diag.py>` | `main:function` (line 877-989) |\n| `scripts/dead-code-prospector-run.py` | `<module:>` | `main:function` (line 659-725) |\n| `scripts/backfill-training-data-cohort-tags.py` | `<module:>` | `main:function` (line 200-244) |\n| `scripts/per_package_fallback.py` | `<module:>` | `main:function` (line 138-166) |\n| `scripts/measure-playbook-overlap.py` | `<module:>` | `main:function` (line 125-176) |\n\nSix scripts × 2 entries = 12 of the 17 reported entries. Only 5 unique main_functions don't follow this pattern (e.g., `__main__.py` files in package roots that don't have a separate `main()` function).\n\n**Why this is a problem.**\n\nTwo consumer-visible failures:\n\n1. **Count is wrong.** An agent reading `len([e for e in entrypoints if e.kind == \"main_function\"])` gets 17, infers \"17 main entrypoints,\" but the repo really has 11 distinct executable scripts. Cross-checks against `find scripts/ -executable | xargs grep -l 'if __name__'` will disagree.\n\n2. **No way to dedupe.** The two entries for the same script have different `symbol_id` (one points at the module, one at the function), different `label`, and there's no field tying them together. A consumer can't programmatically collapse them without parsing symbol_ids and applying ad-hoc logic.\n\n**Root cause.** The entrypoint detector flags both `__main__` blocks and `main` functions as entrypoints independently, with no deduplication step that recognizes \"the module's main-guard calls this main() function — emit one entrypoint, not two.\"\n\n**Invariant statement.** Each distinct executable script must produce exactly one entrypoint entry. If both a `if __name__ == \"__main__\"` block and a `main()` function are present and the block calls the function, the entrypoint should point at the function (canonical \"real\" entrypoint) with the module-level main-guard noted as supporting evidence in `metadata` or similar.\n\n**Severity.** Medium. Misleads consumers about executable surface area. For hypergumbo specifically the over-count is 17 vs 11 (54% inflation). Worse on repos with many CLI scripts.\n\n**Fix proposal.**\n\nAfter both detectors run, deduplicate: for each script with both a module-level `__main__` entry and a `main()` function entry, where the main-guard contains a call to `main()`, keep only the function-level entry and drop the module-level one (or merge them, recording the main-guard line in the function entry's metadata).\n\nIf the main-guard calls something else (not `main`), keep both entries — they're genuinely different entrypoints.\n\n**Test gap.** Property test on self-analysis fixture: `len(set(e.symbol_id.split(\":\")[1] for e in entrypoints if e.kind == \"main_function\")) == len([e for e in entrypoints if e.kind == \"main_function\"])` — assert script-level uniqueness of main_function entrypoints.\n\n**Cross-references.**\n\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/` — entrypoint detector (location TBD; need to grep `main_function`)\n- `INV-ronuf-...` (filed round 6 — phantom file Symbols, sibling \"duplicate entries\" pattern but different mechanism)\n- `INV-fusus-...` (filed round 4 — stable_id collisions, also an identity-dedup issue)\n- `WI-fozoh-...` (done — duplicate file Symbols, related but resolved)"  # 104c
    fields:  # 104c
      statement: "Each distinct executable script must produce exactly one entrypoint entry. If both an 'if __name__ == \"__main__\"' block and a main() function are present and the block calls the function, the entrypoint should point at the function (canonical real entrypoint) with the module-level main-guard noted as supporting evidence in metadata or similar."  # 104c
      root_cause: "The entrypoint detector flags both __main__ blocks (kind=main_function, label='Python script (if __name__ == \"__main__\")', on module Symbol) and main() functions (kind=main_function, label='Python main()', on function Symbol) as entrypoints independently, with no deduplication step that recognizes 'the module main-guard calls this main() function, emit one entry not two.'"  # 104c
